<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2015/06/nuspec.xsd">
	<metadata>
		<id>drift</id>
		<version>@version@</version>
		<packageSourceUrl>https://github.com/gmitch215/Drift/tree/v@version@/packaging/chocolatey/drift</packageSourceUrl>
		<owners>gmitch215</owners>
		<title>Drift</title>
		<authors>Gregory Mitchell</authors>
		<projectUrl>https://github.com/gmitch215/Drift</projectUrl>
		<iconUrl>https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/gmitch215/Drift@v@version@/assets/drift.png</iconUrl>
		<copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 Gregory Mitchell</copyright>
		<licenseUrl>https://github.com/gmitch215/Drift/blob/v@version@/LICENSE</licenseUrl>
		<requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
		<projectSourceUrl>https://github.com/gmitch215/Drift/tree/v@version@</projectSourceUrl>
		<docsUrl>https://github.com/gmitch215/Drift/blob/v@version@/ADVANCED_USAGE.md</docsUrl>
		<bugTrackerUrl>https://github.com/gmitch215/Drift/issues</bugTrackerUrl>
		<tags>drift kotlin diagnostics reproducibility cli</tags>
		<summary>Diagnose why a program passes in one environment and fails in another</summary>
		<description>Drift is the command line tool of a diagnosis engine for a program that passes in one environment and fails in another. It records each environment as a capsule, ranks what differs between a passing run and a failing one, proposes the experiment that would remove the most uncertainty, and writes a certificate that a second command can verify.

The package installs the native `drift` executable for Windows x64 from the GitHub release and puts it on the PATH through a Chocolatey shim. Run `drift --help` to list the commands. `drift solve` needs Docker; every other command runs without it.

The desktop app is the separate `drift-studio` package.</description>
		<releaseNotes>https://github.com/gmitch215/Drift/releases/tag/v@version@</releaseNotes>
	</metadata>
	<files>
		<file src="tools\**" target="tools" />
	</files>
</package>
